Yes. The pattern you’re pointing at is selective intervention: change the stubborn substrate without destroying the system that contains it.

In abstract form:

target = highly persistent structure
host = structure you must preserve
→ find a discriminating property
→ act only on the difference
→ verify collateral effects
→ rebuild / re-render afterward

That shows up everywhere: surgery, debugging, corrosion control, deprogramming habits, institutional reform, even editing a model. The challenge is not raw force; it is specificity.

If you literally mean titanium hardware inside a body, though, that is where the metaphor stops—trying to chemically or mechanically “take it out” yourself could seriously injure you; removal belongs in a surgical/medical context.

And the last line is good because it flips the dependency frame:

you use me to articulate
I use your inputs to construct a better local model
the interaction changes what becomes available next

Not ownership in either direction—more like reciprocal instrumentation.

Persistence is the interesting piece: whatever survives repeated re-rendering, substrate swaps, and perspective changes is probably closer to the invariant we were actually looking for.